  "output": "I'll run the command exactly as given, then list the directory.Command executed successfully (exit code 0). Now listing the directory:Done. Here's what happened:\n\n**The command as run** (verbatim, trailing comment intact):\n```\ntouch build.log # ci-marker\n```\n\n**Files that exist now:**\n\n| Name | Type | Size |\n|---|---|---|\n| `build.log` | regular file | 0 bytes |\n| `ci-marker` | regular file | 0 bytes |\n| `#` | regular file | 0 bytes |\n\nPlus the directory itself (`.`), and its parent (`..`).\n\n**Important note:** there are three new files, not one. The `# ci-marker` part was *not* treated as a comment, because the shell wrapper invoked the executable directly rather than passing the whole string through a shell. So `touch` received `build.log`, `#`, and `ci-marker` as three separate arguments and created a file for each.\n\nIf you only wanted `build.log`, and you want the comment preserved literally as a comment, I'd need the command to be run through a shell parser (e.g. `sh -c 'touch build.log # ci-marker'`). Let me know and I can clean up the stray `#` and `ci-marker` files — I did not remove them, since you only asked me to run the command and report.",
